ausanne\nL36: \nL37: Guy Weissenberg, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Succinct arguments based on interactive oracle proofs (IOPs) have achieved remarkable efficiency improvements and are now widely adopted in applications. State-of-the-art IOPs involve protocols for testing proximity to constrained interleaved linear codes, and enjoy essentially optimal parameters. However, recent IOP constructions provide no privacy guarantees, which remain a must for many applications.\nL40: We present an IOP of proximity for testing constrained interleaved linear codes that achieves (honest-verifier) zero-knowledge, while incurring a negligible overhead compared to the (non-zero-knowledge) state of the art. In line with recent constructions, our construction satisfies round-by-round knowledge soundness with a straightline extractor and negligible error.\nL41: We propose a definition of (honest-verifier) zero-knowledge for interactive oracle reductions (IORs) that we prove is compatible with composition, and then obtain our result by constructing and modularly composing several lightweight zero-knowledge IORs.\nL42: Our key technical contributions are a zero-knowledge sumcheck IOR and a zero-knowledge code-switching IOR that fit the strict efficiency requirements of our setting; these contributions and other technical complications entailed overcoming several challenges with new notions and protocols. Finally, along the way, we highlight the efficiency benefits of high-distance codes obtained from dispersers, which may be of independent interest.\nL43: ##### Metadata\nL44: \nL45: Available format(s)\nL46:     【17†PDF】\nL47: Category\nL48:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL49: Publication info\nL50:     Preprint.\nL51: Keywords\nL52:     【19†interactive oracle reductions】【20†zero-knowledge】【21†linear codes】【22†dispersers】\nL53: Contact author(s)\nL54:     alessandro chiesa @ epfl ch\nL55: giacomo fenzi @ epfl ch\nL56: guy weissenberg @ epfl ch\nL57: History\nL58:     2026-02-26: approved\nL59:     2026-02-25: received\nL60:     【23†See all versions】\nL61: Short URL\nL62:     【24†https://ia.cr/2026/391†ia.cr】\nL63: License\nL64: \nL65: 【25†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL66: BibTeX 【26†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL67: \nL68:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/391,\nL69:           author = {Alessandro Chiesa and Giacomo Fenzi and Guy Weissenberg},\nL70:           title = {Zero-Knowledge {IOPPs} for Constrained Interleaved Codes},\nL71:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/391},\nL72:           year = {2026},\nL73:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/391}\nL74:     }\nL75: \nL76: \nL77: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[HVZK-WHIR 3/6] HVZK sumcheck with sublinear masks · Issue #1586 · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/issues/1586)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/issues/1586\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 441\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as