†#1590】. Paper: 【77†eprint 2026/391, Section 6 / Construction 6.3†eprint.iacr.org】. Depends on 【78†#1584】 (ZK encoding).\nL165: ## What\nL166: \nL167: This is the core technical contribution of the paper. WHIR's sumcheck reduces a claim about an interleaved code to a claim about its base code; the HVZK variant additionally sends `k` small mask polynomials encoded with a ZK code (issue 1), then folds them into the sumcheck claim with one combination challenge `ε`. Per-round message size grows by `Õ(λ)` field elements, independent of the witness size — this is what gives the paper its `1 + o(1)` overhead.\nL168: Construction 6.3 is fully spelled out. Lemma 6.4 gives the simulator and HVZK error; Lemma 6.5 gives RBR knowledge soundness.\nL169: ## Where\nL170: \nL171: `whir/src/sumcheck/zk.rs` (new), mirroring `single.rs`. Gate behind a const generic on the existing sumcheck types so the non-ZK path is byte-for-byte unchanged.\nL172: ## API sketch\nL173: \nL174:     pub struct ZkSumcheckProver<F, EF, Enc>\nL175:     where\nL176:         F: Field,\nL177:         EF: ExtensionField<F>,\nL178:         Enc: ZkEncoding<F>,\nL179:     {\nL180:         base: WhirSumcheckProver<F, EF>,\nL181:         encoding: Enc,\nL182:         masks: Vec<UnivariatePolynomial<F>>,\nL183:         mask_oracles: Vec<Enc::Codeword>,\nL184:     }\nL185: \nL186:     impl<F, EF, Enc: ZkEncoding<F>> ZkSumcheckProver<F, EF, Enc> {\nL187:         pub fn commit_masks<R: Rng>(&mut self, rng: &mut R);     // step 1 of Construction 6.3\nL188:         pub fn send_target_mu_tilde(&self) -> EF;                // step 2\nL189:         pub fn round(&mut self, gamma: EF, eps: EF) -> UnivariatePolynomial<EF>; // step 4\nL190:     }\nL191: ## Acceptance criteria\nL192:   * proptest: completeness across many `(k, ℓ_zk)` configurations.\nL193:   * proptest: HVZK simulator (Lemma 6.4) matches the real view exactly for RS-based encoding (error 0). Use the `simulate` from 【78†[HVZK-WHIR 1/6] ZK encoding trait + Reed–Solomon instantiation #1584】 for queries to mask oracles.\nL194:   * proptest: an empirical RBR soundness bound matching Lemma 6.5 over a small field.\nL195:   * regression test: with `ZK = false`, the transcript is byte-identical to today's sumcheck.\nL196:   * criterion bench: ZK overhead `≤ 1.05×` for `(2^20 rows, k = 4, λ = 100)`.\nL197: ## Notes\nL198:   * Construction 6.3 requires `char(F) ≠ 2` and `ℓ_zk ≥ 2` (Lemma 6.4 proof sketch). KoalaBear and BabyBear fit; add a `static_assert`.\nL199:   * The simulator argument in Lemma 6.4 counts degrees of freedom in the masked sumcheck space; understanding it is the way to build confidence in the implementation, but the implementation itself does not need that machinery.\nL200:   * API and file layout above are guidelines. If the right factoring turns out different (e.g., merging mask commit and target into one step), raise it in the PR.\nL201: Reactions are currently unavailable\nL202: ## Activity\nL203: \nL204:   1. 【74†tcoratger】\nL205: \nL206: added\nL207: \nL208: 【72†enhancementNew feature or request】New feature or request\nL209: \nL210: 【73†help wantedExtra attention is needed】Extra attention is needed\nL211: \nL212: 【79†WHIR】\nL213: \nL214: 【80†on Apr 28, 2026】\nL215: \nL216:   2. ### camofu commented on Apr 28, 2026\nL217: \nL218: 【81†camofu】\nL219: \nL220: 【82†on Apr 28, 2026】\nL221: \nL222: Contributor\nL223: \nL224: More actions\nL225: \nL226: I will be happy to be assigned to this one. Would be coordinating with 【78†#1584】 on the trait surface\nL227: \nL228:   3. 【74†tcoratger】\nL229: \nL230: assigned\nL231: \nL232: 【81†camofu】\nL233: \nL234: 【83†on Apr 28, 2026】\nL235:   4. ### tcoratger commented on Apr 28, 2026\nL236: \nL237: 【74†tcoratger】\nL238: \nL239: 【84†on Apr 28, 2026】\nL240: \nL241: Collaborator Author\nL242: \nL243: More actions\nL244: \nL245: > I will be happy to be assigned to this one. Would be coordinating with 【78†#1584】 on the trait surface\nL246: \nL247: Assigned :)\nL248: \nL249:   5. ### latifkasuli commented on Apr 28, 2026\nL250: \nL251: 【85†latifkasuli】\nL252: \nL253: 【86†on Apr 28, 2026】\nL254: \nL255: Contributor\nL256: \nL257: More actions\nL258: \nL259: I'd like to take this one.\nL260: I'll start with Section 4 (Theorem 4.5) and Section 6 (Construction 6.3) this week. Plan is to gate behind a const generic so the ZK=false path stays byte-identical, land the simulator-vs-real-view proptests per Lemma 6.4, and bench the overhead target (≤1.05× at 2^20, k=4, λ=100).\nL261: \nL262: Happy to coordinate with 【87†@CarloModicaPortfolio】 on the 【78†#1584】 trait surface.\nL263: \nL264:   6. ### tcoratger commented on Apr 28, 2026\nL265: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2609.36437] Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36437)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36437\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 Sep 2026]\nL9: # Title:Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Taeung Yoon】, 【6†Yupeng Zhang】, 【7†Xiaojing Liao】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Bits Under ZK-LLM: Evaluating Zero-Knowledge-Friendly Quantization for Verifiable Private LLM Inference, by Taeung Yoon and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge proofs are emerging as a promising approach for enabling private, verifiable LLM governance and auditing, where regulators, users, and auditors need to verify claims about training-data usage or LLM inference-time behavior, while model providers must protect proprietary model parameters. However, despite the growing interest in ZK-LLMs, the understanding of ZK-friendly quantization remains limited.\nL17: This gap matters because in the ZK setting, quantization directly shapes the arithmetic structure, constraint complexity, and proving cost of ZK inference. ZK protocols operate over finite fields and incur costs that depend heavily on the number and type of arithmetic operations, nonlinearities, and lookup constraints. Understanding ZK-friendly quantization is therefore essential for making ZK-LLMs practical. In this work, we present the first systematic study of ZK-friendly quantization for LLMs.\nL18: We first formalize the definition of ZK-friendly quantization, capturing the properties required for ZK proof generation. We then evaluate nine language models, including Qwen2.5-14B and the mixture-of-experts model Qwen3-30B-A3B, across a broad design space of weight, activation, and nonlinear lookup table precision. Our results show that activation precision is substantially more sensitive than weight precision, while nonlinear lookup approximations can become the dominant source of utility degradation.\nL19: Also, we identify RMSNorm inverse-square-root lookups as a recurring bottleneck in several large models and recover near-baseline utility by selectively increasing precision only at the bottleneck. Finally, we show that reducing bit-width or lookup-table size does not necessarily yield proportional end-to-end proving savings, showing that conventional low-bit quantization heuristics do not directly translate to ZK proving efficiency and motivating operator-aware precision selection.\nL20: Comments:  | 22 pages, 5 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2609.36437】 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2609.36437v1】 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.